<DocsLayout title="Case Study: Black October" description="How PressHaven is exposed to and protected against PHP2Shell and Node2Shell, including deployments still running older PHP or Node inside their containers" section="AI-Era Defense" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    <strong>Black October</strong> (October 2026) is the first wave the rest of this section was written for: a
    cluster of critical, AI-accelerated disclosures in software almost every web stack runs, exploited within
    days. Two of them matter directly to a PressHaven deployment: <strong>PHP2Shell</strong> in the PHP runtime
    that every WordPress container runs, and <strong>Node2Shell</strong> in the Brotli decoder bundled with
    Node.js, which Kibana runs. This page states plainly what is exposed, what the existing layers do and don't
    cover, and what to change, including when an image can't be upgraded straight away.
  </p>

  <div class="admonition warning">
    <div class="admonition-title">Short version</div>
    <p>PressHaven now runs patched PHP: every WordPress container is WordPress 7.1.3 on PHP 8.4.27 under Apache
    <code>mod_php</code>. Until the end of October it stayed on PHP 8.1.33 <em>on purpose</em>, protected only by
    three interim layers: a <a href="#old">hardened WordPress image</a>, a <a href="#guard">request body guard</a>
    at the edge and a <a href="#quarantine">parser-exploit quarantine</a>. All three stay in place as defence in
    depth. Kibana is on 9.5.5, but its bundled Node 24.21.0 is still below the 24.22.1 fix. It was checked again
    and doesn't decode Brotli request bodies.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="summary">What Happened</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard title="PHP2Shell" icon="alert" tag="CVE-2026-109618 / 109619" description="Remote code execution in PHP 5.4–8.5's request-body handling, triggered before any application code runs. Fixed in 8.2.35, 8.3.36, 8.4.27 and 8.5.12. Only source backports exist for 7.4–8.1." meta="pre-auth RCE" />
    <DocCard title="Node2Shell" icon="alert" tag="CVE-2026-111497" description="Out-of-bounds write in the Brotli decoder bundled with Node, reachable through node:zlib Brotli decompression of attacker data. Fixed in 22.23.4, 24.22.1 and 26.12.1. No fix for Node 20 or 25." meta="decompression RCE" />
  </CardGrid>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CVE-2026-109618.</strong> PHP runs out of memory while it parses <code>$_POST</code> or a
    multipart body. Deeply nested array field names push it to <code>memory_limit</code>, which leaves a hash
    bucket uninitialised. That bucket is visited again during shutdown, and the attacker controls what's in it.</li>
    <li><strong>CVE-2026-109619.</strong> Stale per-worker state survives into the next request on the same
    worker, giving a use-after-free. It can be reached through file uploads and session upload progress
    (<code>mod_php</code> and FPM alike, needs a reused worker). On 8.5 it can also be reached through
    <code>fatal_error_backtraces</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Exploitation.</strong> Every known trigger is a multipart request body. Exploitation was seen
    from 17 October, and watchTowr confirmed active attempts. A successful exploit can return
    <strong>HTTP 200</strong>, so status codes won't show it.</li>
    <li><strong>Node2Shell.</strong> No official workaround exists. The only interim advice is to stop handing
    untrusted Brotli data (<code>Content-Encoding: br</code>) to an affected Node process.</li>
    <li><strong>Around them.</strong> The same weeks brought the eMudhra CA compromise, a LiteLLM supply-chain
    attack (PyPI 1.107.0 and its Docker images) and a CVSS 10 GitLab RCE. None of these ship in PressHaven, but
    they shape the <a href="#supply">supply-chain lessons</a> below.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="exposure">Exposure in This Project</h2>
  <p>Checked against the running containers of the current compose files:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Component</th><th>Image / runtime</th><th>PHP2Shell</th><th>Node2Shell</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Edge (<code>openresty/openresty:1.31.1.1-5-alpine</code>)</td><td>OpenResty 1.31.1.1, OpenSSL 3.5.9</td><td>Not affected: no PHP</td><td>Not affected: no Node, no Brotli module, nginx doesn't decompress request bodies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Production storefront</td><td><code>presshaven/wordpress:7.1.3-php8.4-hardened</code> (built from <code>wordpress:7.1.3-php8.4-apache</code> by digest): PHP 8.4.27, Apache 2.4.69, <code>mod_php</code> prefork</td><td><strong>Patched.</strong> The <a href="#old">hardened image</a> and the <a href="#guard">body guard</a> stay as defence in depth</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decoy pools (<code>honeypot_eshop_N</code> and pool_manager's runtime pools)</td><td>Same hardened image as production</td><td>Patched, same extra layers</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
    <tr><td>wp-cli sidecars</td><td><code>wordpress:cli-2.12.0-php8.4</code></td><td>Patched (PHP 8.4.27), and not reachable over HTTP anyway</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Kibana (SIEM host)</td><td><code>kibana:9.5.5</code>, bundled Node 24.21.0</td><td>n/a</td><td><strong>Vulnerable runtime</strong> (fix is 24.22.1, no Kibana release ships it yet), but Brotli request bodies are not decoded (<a href="#kibana">verified</a>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool manager, dashboard</td><td>Python 3.15 (pool manager), 3.12 (dashboard)</td><td>n/a</td><td>No Python <code>brotli</code> package installed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Database</td><td><code>mysql:8.4.13</code> (LTS)</td><td>n/a</td><td>n/a (internal networks only)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Before the upgrade, the stock PHP settings made it worse, which is what the hardened image changes. <code>memory_limit=128M</code>
  is easy to reach while parsing, and <code>session.upload_progress.enabled=1</code> exposes the upload-progress
  path. <code>disable_functions</code> is empty, so a working exploit gets a shell immediately. And the edge keeps
  up to 512 idle keepalive connections to production, so requests from different clients are served one after
  another by the same Apache worker: exactly the worker reuse the 109619 path needs.</p>

  <h2 id="already">What Already Helps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The edge parses nothing.</strong> Every request crosses OpenResty first. It has no PHP and no
    Brotli, so a malformed body can't hurt the proxy itself, and the edge is the right place to
    <a href="#guard">inspect that body</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Full-body buffering is the default.</strong> The config never turns off
    <code>proxy_request_buffering</code>, so nginx receives the whole body, chunked bodies included, before
    passing anything to PHP. A guard at the edge therefore sees the same bytes PHP will parse.</li>
    <li><strong>Behavioural diversion.</strong> Clients that probe first, run scanners, upload suspicious files
    or come from bad-reputation IPs are diverted to a pool <em>before</em> their exploit request, as described in
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/deception#sensor">Deception</a>. Mass PHP2Shell scanning mostly comes from exactly
    those clients.</li>
    <li><strong>Upload scoring.</strong> <code>upload_rules.lua</code> already scores multipart requests and
    suspicious file names on the <code>admin-ajax.php</code> upload path.</li>
    <li><strong>Containment pieces.</strong> Every pool has its own database credentials. WordPress containers
    have <code>mem_limit</code> set. The SIEM sits on a separate host behind an outbound-only mTLS channel, so an
    exploited storefront can't erase its own trail.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gaps">What Does Not Help</h2>
  <p>Being honest about these matters more than the list above:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Application-level controls are bypassed.</strong> The bug fires while PHP parses the body,
    before WordPress, its login, its nonces or any plugin runs. Admin gating, <code>DISALLOW_FILE_*</code> and
    the route manifest don't protect anything if the request still reaches PHP. (The manifest helps only if it
    also restricts which routes may receive multipart.)</li>
    <li><strong>Behavioural diversion alone misses first-request exploits.</strong> A single multipart POST from
    a clean IP to <code>/</code> gives stages 3–10 nothing to act on. Before the <a href="#guard">body guard</a>,
    it went straight to vulnerable production PHP.</li>
    <li><strong>Diversion alone sends the exploit to vulnerable PHP too.</strong> The pools run the same PHP, so
    a diverted PHP2Shell would <em>succeed</em> there: a real RCE inside a decoy on the flat pool network. That
    is why parser-shaped bodies are <a href="#quarantine">quarantined</a> rather than diverted.</li>
    <li><strong>The seeded CVE table can't match it.</strong> There's no fixed URL or parameter to sign. The
    shape lives in the body's structure, which only the body guard parses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="now">Do This Now</h2>
  <p>Patching is the fix, and PressHaven has now done it. This is what the upgrade involved, for deployments
  still on the old images:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Move every WordPress service off PHP 8.1.</strong> The hardened image is now built from
    <code>wordpress:7.1.3-php8.4-apache</code> (pinned by digest) and the wp-cli sidecars use
    <code>wordpress:cli-2.12.0-php8.4</code>. Check any tag with <code>docker run --rm &lt;image&gt; php -v</code>
    (at least 8.3.36 or 8.4.27). Roll it through a pool first, using the
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/hardening#updates">pool-first path</a>, then run
    <code>scripts/update_wordpress.sh --apply</code> to migrate the database.</li>
    <li><strong>Upgrade MySQL with it.</strong> 5.7 goes to 8.0 and then 8.4 in place (one LTS step at a time,
    after a dump). The servers keep <code>mysql_native_password</code>
    (<code>--mysql-native-password=ON --authentication-policy=mysql_native_password</code>) because the
    MariaDB-based clients in the wp-cli and Alpine images can't load <code>caching_sha2_password</code>. 8.4 is
    the last series where that works; plan the client switch before 9.x.</li>
    <li><strong>Upgrade Kibana.</strong> Elasticsearch and Kibana go 8.17.3 → 8.19.23 (Upgrade Assistant clean) →
    9.5.5, always on the same version. Check the bundled Node with
    <code>docker run --rm --entrypoint /usr/share/kibana/node/bin/node &lt;image&gt; -v</code>. 9.5.5 bundles
    24.21.0, which is supported but not yet patched, so the <a href="#kibana">Kibana controls</a> still apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the interim layers after upgrading.</strong> The <a href="#old">hardened image</a>'s
    settings and the <a href="#guard">body guard</a> also cover the next parser bug, not just this one. Rebase
    <code>images/wordpress_hardened/Dockerfile</code> onto the new tag rather than dropping it.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="old">If You Must Stay on Old PHP or Node</h2>
  <p>PressHaven stayed on PHP 8.1 for the first weeks of Black October deliberately, to show how far mitigation
  goes without a patched binary. The same layer now sits on top of patched PHP 8.4. All WordPress services in
  both compose files, and pool_manager's runtime pools (<code>POOL_WORDPRESS_IMAGE</code>), run
  <code>presshaven/wordpress:7.1.3-php8.4-hardened</code>, built from <code>ids/images/wordpress_hardened/</code>.
  It is the official image, pinned by digest, plus three files. On an unpatched PHP, use them as shown; the
  <code>memory_limit</code> line below is the unpatched setting:</p>
  <pre><code># /etc/apache2/conf-available/php-black-october.conf  (enabled)
# php_admin_*: web SAPI only, and neither .htaccess nor ini_set() can undo it
&lt;IfModule mod_php.c&gt;
    # 109618: parsing can't stop half-way at memory_limit. The container's
    # cgroup mem_limit (1024m) is the real cap: an OOM-killed child, not a
    # corrupted heap.
    # (On the patched 8.4 image this is back to 512M.)
    php_admin_value memory_limit -1
    # 109619: close the upload-progress path
    php_admin_flag session.upload_progress.enabled off
    # Shrink what the parser builds (max_input_vars stays 1000 for product screens)
    php_admin_value max_input_nesting_level 16
    php_admin_value max_file_uploads 20
    # Make a successful exploit worth less
    php_admin_value disable_functions exec,system,passthru,shell_exec,proc_open,popen,pcntl_exec
&lt;/IfModule&gt;
LimitRequestBody 16777216</code></pre>
  <pre><code># /etc/apache2/conf-available/mpm-black-october.conf  (enabled)
# 109619 needs a worker that served an earlier request. One request per child:
KeepAlive Off
&lt;IfModule mpm_prefork_module&gt;
    MaxConnectionsPerChild 1
    StartServers 10
    MinSpareServers 10
    MaxSpareServers 20
&lt;/IfModule&gt;

; /usr/local/etc/php/conf.d/zz-black-october.ini
expose_php = Off</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Verified on the running containers.</strong> Through Apache, PHP reports
    <code>memory_limit=-1</code> (on 8.1; 512M on 8.4), upload progress off and nesting 16; <code>ini_set()</code> can't change them;
    every request gets a new child PID; responses carry <code>Connection: close</code> and no
    <code>X-Powered-By</code>. The PHP CLI (WP-Cron, wp-cli) keeps the stock settings.</li>
    <li><strong>One request per child closes the cross-request window.</strong> The parent never serves a
    request, so no request can find state a previous one left behind. Opcache is shared memory, so compiled
    scripts survive. Measured cost: a storefront page through the edge still answers in about 120 ms.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not use <code>RLIMIT_AS</code> or <code>RLIMIT_DATA</code></strong> (<code>ulimit -v</code>)
    as the memory cap. Out-of-memory then happens inside the allocator, mid-parse, which brings back the state
    the fix avoids. Keep Docker's cgroup <code>mem_limit</code> and <code>pids_limit</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>The tag is local-only.</strong> <code>production_eshop</code> builds it with
    <code>pull_policy: build</code> and every other service uses <code>pull_policy: never</code>, so a Docker
    Hub image squatting the same name is never pulled. pool_manager uses <code>containers.create()</code>,
    which fails instead of pulling.</li>
    <li><strong>Node.</strong> No setting makes an unpatched Brotli decoder safe (Node 20, or the 24.21.0
    inside Kibana 9.5.5). The interim control is
    <em>reachability</em>: no untrusted Brotli may reach Kibana (see <a href="#kibana">Protecting
    Kibana</a>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="guard">New Layer: Request Body Guard</h2>
  <p>
    The reverse proxy now reads the <em>structure</em> of every request body before anything is proxied. It is a
    positive model, in the spirit of the <a href="/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting#params">parameter shapes</a>:
    describe what a legitimate storefront body looks like, and treat parser-stressing bodies as hostile whatever
    CVE they're for. It follows the project's split: <code>lua/body_guard_rules.lua</code> is pure and unit-tested
    with plain <code>lua</code> (<code>tests/test_body_guard_rules.lua</code>), and <code>lua/body_guard.lua</code>
    is the adapter.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Check</th><th>Limit</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Request <code>Content-Encoding</code> other than absent/<code>identity</code>, or repeated</td><td>Quarantine, score +40</td><td>No browser compresses request bodies. Removes Brotli and gzip-bomb paths for every backend</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Number of multipart parts / urlencoded fields</td><td>≤ 1200</td><td>PHP stops at <code>max_input_vars</code> (1000) anyway; more is hash-table pressure</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Field-name length / bracket depth (<code>a[b][c]…</code>, also <code>%5B</code>-encoded)</td><td>≤ 256 bytes / ≤ 8 levels</td><td>Nested arrays are how 109618 reaches <code>memory_limit</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Field named <code>PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS</code> or <code>PHPSESSID</code> (after PHP's dot/space mangling)</td><td>Quarantine</td><td>The 109619 upload-progress path. No storefront form sends these</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Missing, repeated or non-RFC 2046 boundary; part header without an end; NUL in a part header</td><td>Quarantine</td><td>Anything PHP might split differently from the guard is a parser differential, so it isn't guessed at</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Control bytes in a field name or file name</td><td>Quarantine</td><td>Parser probes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Upload <code>filename</code> containing <code>&lt;?php</code>, <code>&lt;?=</code> or a serialized object</td><td>Quarantine</td><td>Code or object payloads in a name field</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Multipart/urlencoded body over 16 MiB</td><td>Quarantine</td><td>Too large to inspect, and Apache refuses it anyway (<code>LimitRequestBody</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The same name checks on the query string</td><td>Query stripped, score +40</td><td>PHP builds <code>$_GET</code> with the same code</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Every <code>name=</code> and <code>filename=</code> parameter in a part header is checked, not just the
  first, and <code>name</code> never matches the tail of <code>filename</code>. Part headers may end with CRLF or
  bare LF, as PHP accepts both. JSON and other bodies PHP doesn't parse into <code>$_POST</code> pass.</p>
  <pre><code># nginx.conf (443 server)
client_body_buffer_size 1m;      # ordinary posts stay in memory

# Server level, rewrite phase: inherited by EVERY location, including those
# with their own access_by_lua_block (which replaces the server-level one),
# and run before anything proxies the body.
rewrite_by_lua_block &#123;
    require("body_guard").check()
&#125;</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Buffered body, not <code>resty.upload</code>.</strong> <code>resty.upload</code> consumes the
    request socket and would leave nothing to proxy. The adapter calls <code>ngx.req.read_body()</code> and
    scans <code>get_body_data()</code>, or the temp file from <code>get_body_file()</code> when the body spilled.
    <code>proxy_request_buffering</code> stays on (the default), so the guard sees the bytes PHP will see.</li>
    <li><strong>Mode.</strong> <code>BODY_GUARD_MODE</code> in compose: <code>enforce</code> (default),
    <code>observe</code> (log only, no change, no score) or <code>off</code>. Limits and scores live in
    <code>_G.config.body_guard</code> in <code>init.lua</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Fails open.</strong> An error in the guard is logged and the request continues unchanged; the
    hardened image still applies behind it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="quarantine">Parser-Exploit Quarantine</h2>
  <p>
    PressHaven's normal response to an attack is to <a href="/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting#divert">divert, not
    block</a>. Parser exploits need an exception, because the decoy runs the same vulnerable runtime: forwarding
    a PHP2Shell body to a pool runs a real exploit against real PHP. A <strong>quarantine</strong> verdict keeps the
    deception without the execution:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>The body and its framing headers (<code>Content-Type</code>, <code>Content-Encoding</code>,
    <code>Transfer-Encoding</code>) are dropped and the method becomes <code>GET</code>. A hostile query string is
    emptied instead. <strong>No PHP backend, production or pool, ever receives the hostile bytes.</strong></li>
    <li>The request then continues through normal routing, so the client gets the shop's ordinary page for that
    URL. There's no 4xx and no oracle telling it what matched.</li>
    <li>A <code>parser_exploit_quarantined</code> security event goes to the SIEM with the reason, method, URI,
    content type, body size and the body's SHA-256.</li>
    <li>The guard's score (100 for parser-shaped bodies, 40 for the weaker signals) joins the request's risk
    score as <code>body_guard:&lt;reason&gt;</code>. At 100 the request and the rest of the session go to a
    pool, where they're observed as usual, but as harmless GETs.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Checked live through the edge: a normal multipart post reached production unchanged, while an
  upload-progress field, 30-level <code>%5B</code> nesting, a Brotli body, a nested query string and a
  <code>&lt;?php</code> file name each arrived at a pool as a bodyless <code>GET</code>, answered with an
  ordinary 200 page. That turns "the decoy got popped" into "the attempt was recorded, the attacker was tagged,
  and nothing executed", whatever PHP version the containers run.</p>

  <h2 id="kibana">Protecting Kibana (Node2Shell)</h2>
  <p>
    Kibana is less exposed than the storefront. It runs on the SIEM host, behind the firewall, and only analysts
    should reach it. It was also checked directly on 8.17.3, and again on 9.5.5 (where
    <code>POST /api/saved_objects/_bulk_get</code> answers 200 for identity and gzip bodies and 400 for the same
    body sent as <code>br</code>): Kibana's HTTP server registers its Brotli decoder
    only when <code>server.compression.brotli.enabled</code> is set (default off), and even then doesn't
    decompress request bodies. A <code>Content-Encoding: br</code> body is parsed raw, while the same body with
    <code>gzip</code> is decompressed and rejected, so attacker Brotli never reaches the vulnerable decoder. The
    SIEM VM has no internet route and <code>TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false</code>, which removes Brotli
    <em>responses</em> too. Keep it that way until a Kibana release bundles Node 24.22.1 or later:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Don't enable Brotli.</strong> Leave <code>server.compression.brotli.enabled</code> unset.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't expose the published port.</strong> Bind <code>KIBANA_PORT</code> to the analyst VPN or
    loopback interface (<code>"127.0.0.1:$&#123;KIBANA_PORT&#125;:$&#123;KIBANA_PORT&#125;"</code>) instead
    of all interfaces, and reach it over a tunnel.</li>
    <li><strong>Put a reverse proxy in front.</strong> Put Kibana behind a small nginx/OpenResty that rejects any
    request with a <code>Content-Encoding</code> header (<code>if ($http_content_encoding) &#123; return 415;
    &#125;</code>). Browsers never compress request bodies to Kibana, so nothing legitimate breaks. Combine it
    with the <code>ip_whitelist</code> ranges you already trust.</li>
    <li><strong>Limit Kibana's outbound traffic.</strong> Brotli can also arrive in <em>responses</em> Kibana
    fetches. Allow it to reach Elasticsearch only, and turn off news-feed and telemetry fetches, so no untrusted
    server can send it a compressed reply.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="detect">Detection Signals</h2>
  <p>PHP2Shell can succeed with an HTTP 200, so watch the host and the body, not just status codes. Each of these
  is a candidate Kibana alert:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Source</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Allowed memory size … exhausted … Unknown on line 0</code></td><td>Apache/PHP error log</td><td>Memory ran out during parsing, before any script ran. That is the 109618 trigger, attempted</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>child pid … exit signal Segmentation fault</code></td><td>Apache error log</td><td>Heap corruption, a failed or partly successful attempt</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>sh</code>/<code>bash</code>/<code>curl</code> spawned by an <code>apache2</code> process</td><td>Container process audit</td><td>Successful RCE. On a pool this is a capture; on production it's an incident</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>parser_exploit_quarantined</code> events, <code>body_guard:*</code> patterns</td><td>Edge (<code>body_guard.lua</code>)</td><td>Attempts: reason, size and SHA-256 of the dropped body</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unexpected <code>Set-Cookie: PHPSESSID</code> from the storefront</td><td>Edge response headers</td><td>WordPress doesn't use PHP sessions, so a session cookie points at the upload-progress path</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Large uploads that stall until <code>client_body_timeout</code></td><td>nginx access log</td><td>Memory-grooming or timing attempts</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Any request with <code>Content-Encoding: br</code></td><td>Edge, Kibana proxy</td><td>Node2Shell probing (legitimate clients don't send it)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Vector already ships the edge logs and security events to the SIEM. The Apache error log and process
  events of the WordPress containers need to be added as sources for the host-side rows.</p>

  <h2 id="supply">Supply-Chain Lessons</h2>
  <p>The other Black October incidents don't touch PressHaven's code, but they apply to how it's deployed:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pin images by digest.</strong> The LiteLLM attack shipped through ordinary version tags.
    PressHaven's former floating tags (<code>openresty/openresty:alpine</code>, <code>alpine:latest</code>,
    <code>jasonish/suricata:latest</code>) are now exact versions. The next step is pinning them by digest, as
    the hardened WordPress base already is, and updating the digest deliberately through the pool-first path.</li>
    <li><strong>Check what's inside an image, not just its tag.</strong> <code>wordpress:6.8.3</code> sounded
    current while it bundled an end-of-life PHP, and even the current <code>kibana:9.5.5</code> bundles a Node
    below the fix. Add
    <code>php -v</code> and <code>node -v</code> checks to your update review.</li>
    <li><strong>Trust stores change too.</strong> After a CA compromise like eMudhra's, rebuilding images
    refreshes their CA bundles. The SIEM's mTLS channel uses its own private CA, so it doesn't depend on the
    public CA set.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> The edge itself isn't affected and already buffered whole bodies, so the body
  guard needed no architectural change. The hardened image covers both CVEs without a patched binary, and its
  settings can't be undone from <code>.htaccess</code> or plugin code. Quarantine keeps deception's benefits
  without running the exploit. The guard's checks describe legitimate storefront bodies rather than this one
  CVE, so they also cover the next parser bug.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> PHP is now patched, but Kibana's bundled Node is not, so its protection is
  still reachability alone. Legitimate multipart (media uploads, checkout file fields) still reaches PHP and
  relies on the per-part limits. The guard parses bodies the way PHP <em>should</em> parse them, and a parser
  differential between the two could evade it. On unpatched PHP, <code>memory_limit=-1</code> trades RCE for
  possible memory-exhaustion DoS, bounded by the container's <code>mem_limit</code>. A route-based multipart allowlist
  would narrow it further but isn't implemented. None of this replaces upgrading, which is why the project
  upgraded too.</p>
